WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.249
- [Narrator] Before there was all of this,

00:00:04.249 --> 00:00:06.330
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:07.290
there was this.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:08.940
- Welcome to "Blockbuster Video".

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.220
- [Narrator] But then
Netflix changed everything.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:12.900
Once a DVD distributor.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:13.780
- It's easy

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.640
- [Narrator] It became a streaming pioneer

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.920
revolutionizing the way people watch TV

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:20.940
and movies.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.820
Other networks and
studios raced to catch up.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.210
And now Disney, HBO and
NBC are chipping away

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:28.830
at Netflix's market.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.600
And in April, 2022.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.060
- Report from Netflix
saying it's lost subscribers

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.740
For the first time in a decade.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:35.573
- [Narrator] That was enough

00:00:35.573 --> 00:00:38.910
to send its stock plummeting
35% in a single day.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.670
Netflix's worst day since 2004.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:43.860
And though the stock
hasn't fully recovered,

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.110
the company made gains
in the third quarter

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.690
adding 2.4 million subscribers.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.090
So what happened to the
streaming giant this year

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:53.280
and how is it bouncing back?

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.830
Netflix lost 200,000 subscribers

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:57.990
in the first quarter of 2022

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:01.290
instead of the 2.5 million
it projected to gain.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.090
Netflix said that completely shutting down

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:05.490
its service in Russia
following the country's attack

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:08.010
on Ukraine resulted in the net loss.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.690
The company also attributed the loss

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:11.880
to increased subscriber churn.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.560
Part of that came from a price increase

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:14.520
in January.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:15.720
- It wasn't the first time

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.100
that Netflix had raised prices

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.440
but it was a price increase

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:22.890
that triggered new sort
of consumer defections.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:24.420
- [Narrator] Along with
increased competition

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.400
and inflation, it also had to contend

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.920
with another problem, password sharing.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.851
- These are over a
hundred million households

00:01:30.851 --> 00:01:33.450
that already are choosing to view Netflix.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.010
They love the service.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:38.100
We just gotta get paid
at some degree for them.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.120
- [Narrator] So the streamer moved

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.140
to make a change.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.480
- We're not trying to
shut down that sharing

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.060
but we're gonna ask you to pay a bit more

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:45.987
to be able to share.

00:01:45.987 --> 00:01:47.640
- [Narrator] And that wasn't all.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.680
As the company tried to control costs,

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.960
it laid off more than 450 people

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.370
over the next few months.

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.470
- The stock decline triggered a series

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:58.080
of changes that the
company is still enacting.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.360
So one of the biggest changes that came

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:01.770
after that decline that was announced

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.150
that same day was that Netflix

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.700
would add an ad supported tier of service.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.470
- [Narrator] But those
changes aren't about face

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:10.110
for the streamer which
has famously assured ads

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:11.490
and embraced sharing.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.140
- Those who have followed Netflix know

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.090
that I've been against the complexity

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.790
of advertising and a big
fan of the simplicity

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:19.050
of subscription.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.400
- [Narrator] That simplicity worked

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:21.870
in the company's favor at a time

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.460
when growing was the goal.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.230
- In the early days of the rise

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.540
of streaming and really the streaming wars

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.580
what helped convince consumers

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:30.870
to cut their cable bill was

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.090
that Netflix was flexible

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.430
and you could binge series
whenever you wanted.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:36.720
You weren't encumbered with ads

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.546
in the same way that you were on cable

00:02:38.546 --> 00:02:41.070
and you could share what you paid

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:42.990
for with family and friends.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:44.220
- [Narrator] And in 2020,

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.020
that model really worked

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.120
especially during the pandemic.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.950
The company's vast content library

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:52.590
and binge able series
drew in customers confined

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:53.640
to their homes.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.670
The streamer's share price rose from $319

00:02:56.670 --> 00:03:01.380
in March of 2020 to
over $550 in early 2021.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.260
It also added a record
37 million new users

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.660
in 2020 on the strength of popular shows

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:07.977
like "The Queens Gambit",

00:03:09.607 --> 00:03:10.440
"Tiger King".

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.690
- My name's Joe Exotic, and this is Sarge.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:13.523
- [Narrator] And "Bridgerton".

00:03:13.523 --> 00:03:15.090
- I wish to be entertained.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:16.620
- There's a subset of companies

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:19.020
that benefited intensely from the pandemic

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.750
and the way people's
lives changed during it.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:24.300
And a commonality that you see

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.030
in those companies is
that when times are good

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:31.020
it's very hard to imagine
times becoming bad again.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.580
- [Narrator] But things did get bad

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.560
as the pandemic wore on and people started

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:35.640
to leave their homes.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.700
In 2021, the company's
growth began to slow.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.380
- COVID created a lot of noise

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.090
in how to read the situation,

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:44.310
boosted us a lot in 2020.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:49.290
And then in 2021, I think
we thoughtfully said

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.143
it was mostly pull forward

00:03:51.143 --> 00:03:54.120
which was the logical conclusion.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:55.770
But now coming into 2022

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:57.360
that you know doesn't really hold.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.640
- [Narrator] April wasn't
the first time in 2022

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.230
that Netflix's stock sank.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:03.570
In January, after the company forecasted

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:05.220
slower subscriber growth,

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.070
shares fell 20% in after hours trading.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:09.030
- In the streaming business,

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:10.800
the number of subscribers
that are paying you

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.680
to watch your content is really one

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.180
of the most important metrics

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.200
of a company's health.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.460
- [Narrator] But while streaming services

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:19.890
like Disney plus or
HBO Max have other ways

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.210
of generating revenue--

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:22.890
- Netflix almost entirely is

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:24.000
in the business of streaming.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.650
And so that is, its lifeblood.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:28.050
And when it loses streaming subscribers

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:30.990
that is a problem for the
heart of its business.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:32.100
- [Narrator] And with a crowded field

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.110
of streamers, gaining new subscribers

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:34.950
can be difficult.

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:36.900
- The challenge that all
the streaming companies

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:39.150
are having now, particularly in the US

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.280
is they have acquired
most of the customers

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:44.400
that are ever going to pay to stream.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.110
They have to figure out
how to make more money

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:48.510
from those people than
what that puts front

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:50.220
and center for all of these services

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.290
is the importance of competing on content.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:53.910
- [Narrator] After April stock decline,

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:56.040
the company scrambled to act quickly.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.600
- There have been rivals that have been

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.120
in the business of
selling ad supported tiers

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:01.230
for quite some time.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:03.690
There have been other streaming services,

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:05.130
Spotify for one that has

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:06.150
always been very stringent

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:07.170
about password sharing.

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:08.970
So these are levers that existed

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:10.860
before that Netflix hadn't pulled.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.150
And so I think that moment sort

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:15.390
of raises the question
of was Netflix too slow

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:17.580
to do all that it could have

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.830
to avoid the severity of this moment?

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:21.720
- [Narrator] This year,
Netflix's share price

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:23.730
is down more than 60%

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:25.260
but the streamer reported something

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:27.300
of a turnaround in the September quarter.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.460
The company added twice
as many subscribers

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:30.840
as expected in Q3,

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:33.030
sending shares up in after hours trading

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:34.710
immediately after its report.

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:37.200
- Thank God we're done
with shrinking quarters.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.090
Everything the company's focused on

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:40.620
whether that's on the content side,

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:44.708
on marketing, lowering
prices to the ad supported,

00:05:44.708 --> 00:05:47.700
the paid sharing, the
thoughtful approach we're

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.400
doing there lines us up

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:51.870
for a good next year.

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:53.370
- [Narrator] Looking
ahead, the streamer says

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:55.350
it's new ad supported tier will launch

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:57.720
in November for 6.99 a month,

00:05:57.720 --> 00:05:59.250
and the company this week laid

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:01.170
out some of the steps
it will take to crack

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:03.660
down on password sharing
and make it easier

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:05.280
for subscribers sharing accounts

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:06.570
to make their own.

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:08.850
- Those are the two strategic elements

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:10.860
that it really needs to nail at this point

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:14.310
because those will prove to shareholders

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:17.250
whether or not the the
promises and decisions

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:18.960
that executives have made are going

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:20.520
to bear fruit and set the company

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:23.227
on a more sustainable path in the future.

00:06:23.227 --> 00:06:25.810
(upbeat music)

